                           HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   “They bought the silence,” said Mama Tete.
   “And in 2024, the whole catalog transferred to Aperture Civic.” Sofía
looked up. “It’s on a permit. A ‘Heritage Sound Archive.’ Climate-con‐
trolled storage annex.” She tapped a line. “Location: lower level, Kings‐
bridge Armory.”
   Nobody said anything. Doña Aurora, at the doorway, set her tray
very quietly on the sideboard.
   “So I know where to go,” said Mama Tete.
   “Mama Tete.” Sofía had her hand up, palm out. “I understand. I do.
But if we break in, we lose everything we’ve got. I need chain of cus‐
tody. I need a subpoena.”
   “I don’t need a subpoena.” Tete laid one hand flat on the photo‐
graph. “I need a key.”
   They stared at each other across the wall, the botánica owner and
the girl with the clicker, and Sofía had finally met somebody a spread‐
sheet wasn’t going to move.
   “Not yet,” said Rafa, quietly, from the corner, where nobody had
known he’d been listening.
   Mama Tete took her hand off the photograph. She didn’t say yes. She
didn’t say no.



The last thing Sofía had was the thing she’d been saving.
   “I found the follow-up,” she said. “The 2023 longitudinal update to
Early Calm. It’s in a university’s open repository, because some grad
student uploaded it and nobody caught it.” She spread three pages.
“They tracked the cohort. All four schools. Six hundred and twelve
kids. Calm Index, level one through four, and outcomes up to age
twenty-two.”
   The table was on page two.

   Level 1: justice-system contact by age 22: 9%
   Level 2: 29%
   Level 3: 68%
   Level 4: 81%




